1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
A military jet pushes the envelope of innovation, but at a deadly cost.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
A North Sea oil platform is devoured by flames in minutes.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
The worst construction accident in U.S. history takes seconds.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:22,000
A deluxe high-speed train derails, and computer errors nearly cause the unthinkable.

5
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Now, Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:39,800
It was a startling leap forward in aviation.

7
00:00:40,140 --> 00:00:47,880
An airplane that could rise from a standstill, hover, fly like a conventional jet, and then land on a dime.

8
00:00:48,720 --> 00:00:54,420
When a plane that big comes straight down and lands right in front of you, it's very impressive.

9
00:00:55,460 --> 00:00:57,760
The Harrier AV-8 attack jet.

10
00:00:57,761 --> 00:01:02,220
an aircraft flown and maintained by the U.S. Marine Corps Aviation Wing.

11
00:01:03,240 --> 00:01:06,540
Its unique feat of engineering and piloting is called VSTOL,

12
00:01:07,220 --> 00:01:09,260
vertical and short takeoff and landing.

13
00:01:10,020 --> 00:01:13,360
It's meant to support Marine ground troops deep in the combat zone,

14
00:01:13,620 --> 00:01:16,760
where runways might be damaged or non-existent.

15
00:01:18,140 --> 00:01:22,000
But the plane's VSTOL capability has come at a terrible price.

16
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:29,760
measured by its major accident rate per 100,000 flight hours which is the military standard

17
00:01:29,761 --> 00:01:35,980
the harrier is the most dangerous plane in the u.s military overall the marines have lost more than

18
00:01:35,981 --> 00:01:44,560
one-third of the entire harrier fleet to accidents 148 major non-combat accidents in its 30 plus

19
00:01:44,561 --> 00:01:49,089
years of service. Billions spent on repairs alone.

20
00:01:49,090 --> 00:01:52,260
Worst of all, 45 Marines, including

21
00:01:52,261 --> 00:01:56,160
some of the Corps' finest aviators, killed during peacetime.

22
00:01:56,161 --> 00:02:06,440
In Marine Corps aviation, I don't know of another aircraft that took the toll of time,

23
00:02:06,940 --> 00:02:12,000
money, and lives to perfect its operation.

24
00:02:12,001 --> 00:02:19,200
Unlike most engineering disasters, the Harrier's failures did not occur in a single catastrophic event,

25
00:02:19,201 --> 00:02:22,480
but rather over the fleet's long lifetime.

26
00:02:24,240 --> 00:02:27,400
And while the Marine Corps has stuck by the Harrier for over 30 years,

27
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:32,440
the need for an airplane like it can be traced as far back as 1942

28
00:02:32,441 --> 00:02:35,980
and the fierce jungle combat on Guadalcanal.

29
00:02:36,860 --> 00:02:41,120
The Marines felt that the Navy left them to battle the Japanese and malaria

30
00:02:41,121 --> 00:02:44,080
and lost a thousand men in that combat.

31
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:48,300
Since then, the precept that the Marines in the air

32
00:02:48,301 --> 00:02:50,180
should protect Marines on the ground

33
00:02:50,181 --> 00:02:52,840
has been a central part of the Corps' ethos.

34
00:02:53,520 --> 00:02:56,820
The Marines continued to fight some of the fiercest battles in U.S. history.

35
00:02:57,900 --> 00:03:00,380
And they continued their search for an attack plane

36
00:03:00,381 --> 00:03:03,300
that could provide close air support in the worst of conditions.

37
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:06,760
Flown by Marines and for Marines.

38
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:10,480
In the 1960s, they thought they'd finally found it

39
00:03:10,481 --> 00:03:14,480
in an experimental British aircraft called the P1127,

40
00:03:14,481 --> 00:03:18,480
soon to be renamed the Harrier, after a bird of prey.

41
00:03:20,480 --> 00:03:22,480
So when the British developed the Harrier,

42
00:03:22,481 --> 00:03:24,480
the Marines were immediately interested,

43
00:03:24,481 --> 00:03:29,480
and in fact one Marine general who flew the plane early on

44
00:03:29,481 --> 00:03:32,480
described it as an answer to a prayer.

45
00:03:32,481 --> 00:03:36,480
In 1970, the Marines placed a large order for the jets

46
00:03:36,481 --> 00:03:39,480
with a British manufacturer, Hawker Sidley.

47
00:03:39,481 --> 00:03:47,320
By May 1971, active marine units began flying the Harrier, now called the AV-8A.

48
00:03:47,560 --> 00:03:54,320
The AV-8A was a notoriously unstable plane which had a tendency to roll over and slam into the ground.

49
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:58,720
It had an astronomical major accident rate.

50
00:03:59,480 --> 00:04:03,320
One month after being rushed into duty, the first fatal crash occurred.

51
00:04:03,321 --> 00:04:08,900
collapsing landing gear fuel tank malfunctions and jammed control sticks

52
00:04:08,901 --> 00:04:13,580
were among the causes of fatal crashes in addition to malfunctioning equipment

53
00:04:13,581 --> 00:04:20,120
the AV-8A presented steep challenges for pilots while manning the control stick

54
00:04:20,121 --> 00:04:24,800
rudder pedals and throttle a Harrier pilot also had to learn to control the

55
00:04:24,801 --> 00:04:30,140
four nozzles that provide the thrust for vertical flight all while keeping an eye

56
00:04:30,141 --> 00:04:30,886
on the wind.

57
00:04:30,887 --> 00:04:36,360
There's no doubt that under the best of circumstances the Harrier is a challenging plane

58
00:04:36,361 --> 00:04:40,626
to fly. I am always very skeptical when the U.S.

59
00:04:40,627 --> 00:04:44,980
military blames something on pilot error. Far too

60
00:04:44,981 --> 00:04:47,603
often that's not the real cause.

61
00:04:47,604 --> 00:04:53,260
The real cause is in the design and if the design is a very difficult

62
00:04:53,261 --> 00:04:59,020
designed to work with, no amount of training can overcome those weaknesses.

63
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:02,700
The AV-8A was also frustrating for mechanics.

64
00:05:03,200 --> 00:05:08,480
When it came to the heavy maintenance, we found ourselves short in special tools and

65
00:05:08,481 --> 00:05:12,660
special slings and adapters measuring devices for that aircraft.

66
00:05:13,180 --> 00:05:14,060
We just didn't have it.

67
00:05:14,660 --> 00:05:15,920
Early on was a disaster.

68
00:05:16,740 --> 00:05:21,880
The AV-8A's difficult design and persistent mechanical flaws contributed to a disastrous

69
00:05:21,881 --> 00:05:22,660
first decade.

70
00:05:23,260 --> 00:05:28,220
with annual accident rates as high as 57 per 100,000 flight hours.

71
00:05:30,100 --> 00:05:34,980
By comparison, the F-A-18, while a more conventional aircraft,

72
00:05:35,540 --> 00:05:40,500
had an accident rate of only 8 per 100,000 hours in its worst year.

73
00:05:41,220 --> 00:05:46,980
In the early 1980s, the Marine Corps introduced the retooled Harrier AV-8B.

74
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:52,180
Its larger wing was designed to provide better stability in flight.

75
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:58,540
Greater engine air intake and a new avionics suite to improve navigation were also added.

76
00:06:00,280 --> 00:06:05,400
The accident rate for the AVA-B has been considerably lower than the AVA-A,

77
00:06:05,540 --> 00:06:12,140
but it remains far higher than the comparable contemporary attack and fighter jets

78
00:06:12,141 --> 00:06:15,280
flown by the Navy, Air Force, and Marines.

79
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:19,740
The AVA-B was first introduced to the fleet in the mid-1980s,

80
00:06:19,741 --> 00:06:25,100
and by 1996, nearly a quarter of them have been destroyed in accidents.

81
00:06:25,620 --> 00:06:28,620
Half of the Harrier's 148 major accidents

82
00:06:28,621 --> 00:06:31,280
have been connected to problems with its powerful

83
00:06:31,281 --> 00:06:34,300
but temperamental Rolls-Royce vectored thrust engine.

84
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:40,880
Repairs and maintenance are complicated by the fact

85
00:06:40,881 --> 00:06:43,800
that the Harrier's engine is exceedingly difficult to access.

86
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:48,560
To do a full engine change-out on a Harrier

87
00:06:48,561 --> 00:06:50,940
can be over 500 hours of labor,

88
00:06:51,800 --> 00:06:54,640
whereas in other military jets,

89
00:06:54,780 --> 00:06:57,940
it can be a day or a half a day,

90
00:06:58,080 --> 00:07:00,220
part of a day to do the same thing.

91
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:02,500
The Harriers' frequent groundings

92
00:07:02,501 --> 00:07:03,860
in time-intensive repairs

93
00:07:03,861 --> 00:07:06,620
have created a catch-22 for many pilots.

94
00:07:07,280 --> 00:07:09,620
They can't log enough flying hours to stay sharp,

95
00:07:10,120 --> 00:07:11,640
which leads to more accidents,

96
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:14,480
which leads to even less flying time.

97
00:07:14,481 --> 00:07:17,680
In 1997, they brought back

98
00:07:17,681 --> 00:07:20,460
a former training squadron commander,

99
00:07:21,280 --> 00:07:24,520
and he found that some of these pilots, inexperienced pilots,

100
00:07:24,940 --> 00:07:27,420
were getting only four or five hours a month,

101
00:07:28,140 --> 00:07:32,920
which he said was not enough to fly a Cessna, never mind a Harrier.

102
00:07:33,380 --> 00:07:36,700
Renewed scrutiny has focused on the Harrier's record in combat.

103
00:07:37,480 --> 00:07:42,540
In the 1991 Gulf War, the Harrier flew over 3,300 missions,

104
00:07:42,820 --> 00:07:47,020
but also sustained the highest casualty rate of major U.S. combat jets.

105
00:07:47,680 --> 00:07:52,820
Five of the seven AV-8Bs that took enemy fire were destroyed.

106
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:57,340
The Harrier's attrition rate was three times that of the A-10 attack jet

107
00:07:57,341 --> 00:07:59,880
and seven times that of the F-16.

108
00:08:00,900 --> 00:08:04,460
Again, what made the Harrier special also made it vulnerable.

109
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:09,420
Infrared-seeking missiles will see the big engine from the Harrier.

110
00:08:09,421 --> 00:08:10,500
It is very hot.

111
00:08:11,480 --> 00:08:16,260
And so surface-to-air missiles with infrared secrets on them will go right for that engine.

112
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:19,000
During Operation Iraqi Freedom,

113
00:08:19,600 --> 00:08:22,220
the Harrier, now outfitted with laser-guided weapons

114
00:08:22,221 --> 00:08:24,240
that allowed it to attack from higher altitudes,

115
00:08:24,840 --> 00:08:25,760
performed admirably.

116
00:08:26,760 --> 00:08:28,280
None were shot down.

117
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:30,660
But a nagging question remained.

118
00:08:31,220 --> 00:08:32,620
Was the Harrier necessary?

119
00:08:33,720 --> 00:08:36,860
What we saw in Afghanistan and in the war in Iraq

120
00:08:36,861 --> 00:08:40,220
was airstrikes conducted from great distances

121
00:08:40,221 --> 00:08:43,500
with precision-guided bombs and missiles.

122
00:08:43,501 --> 00:08:48,640
So I think a lot of the use that the Harrier got in Afghanistan and in Iraq

123
00:08:48,641 --> 00:08:53,020
was sort of giving it a fair chance to show what it could do,

124
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:59,600
but you'd be hard-pressed to show that we couldn't have done the same things other ways.

125
00:09:00,340 --> 00:09:04,600
While Congress, prompted in part by a series of articles in the Los Angeles Times,

126
00:09:05,840 --> 00:09:08,360
investigates the Harrier's latest spike in crashes,

127
00:09:08,361 --> 00:09:13,700
the future of marine aviation is focused on the plane that will one day replace the Harrier,

128
00:09:13,701 --> 00:09:15,900
the Joint Strike Fighter.

129
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:22,180
The Marines model of the JSF, although it won't have the Harrier's distinctive V-stole ability,

130
00:09:23,040 --> 00:09:25,960
will have short takeoff and vertical landing capability.

131
00:09:27,020 --> 00:09:30,260
And it will be outfitted with advanced flight control software

132
00:09:30,261 --> 00:09:32,960
to take some of the burden from pilots.

133
00:09:33,560 --> 00:09:37,760
An airplane that matches the Marines' mission to fight anywhere, anytime,

134
00:09:38,360 --> 00:09:40,360
and get out alive.

135
00:09:40,361 --> 00:09:45,360
The Marines hope the JSF will surpass the performance of the Harrier

136
00:09:45,361 --> 00:09:52,360
without the same toll in money, material, and most importantly, lives.

137
00:09:52,361 --> 00:09:57,360
The Harrier is still flown by the British Royal Air Force and Navy

138
00:09:57,361 --> 00:10:00,360
and is also used in Spain, Italy, India, and Thailand.

139
00:10:00,361 --> 00:10:05,360
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

140
00:10:05,361 --> 00:10:12,100
July 6th, 1988 was a clear, calm night on the often tumultuous North Sea.

141
00:10:12,900 --> 00:10:17,600
But the calm would be shattered by an engineering disaster of mythic proportions.

142
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:24,960
A hydrocarbon-fueled inferno that would become synonymous with the worst-case scenario for an offshore oil platform.

143
00:10:25,760 --> 00:10:27,760
The platform's name was Piper Alpha.

144
00:10:28,900 --> 00:10:33,900
The magnitude of this disaster was a significant event in human history.

145
00:10:33,901 --> 00:10:42,000
And indeed, the history of the North Sea after it will be forever different because of its occurrence.

146
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:46,300
It was also the greatest loss of lives on an oil or gas platform.

147
00:10:48,020 --> 00:10:55,000
167 men perished, victims of a tragic confluence of design flaw, human error, and bad luck.

148
00:10:56,380 --> 00:11:00,280
Oil was discovered under the cold, rough North Sea in 1969.

149
00:11:01,160 --> 00:11:05,080
Occidental Petroleum, one of the first companies to claim the North Sea fields,

150
00:11:05,660 --> 00:11:08,500
knew that to succeed, it would have to think big.

151
00:11:09,040 --> 00:11:11,200
The result was the Piper Alpha.

152
00:11:12,380 --> 00:11:17,060
This was the first platform of this type and scale in the North Sea.

153
00:11:17,061 --> 00:11:22,080
It cost $1 billion in 1975.

154
00:11:23,200 --> 00:11:26,760
So this was a significant engineering platform.

155
00:11:26,761 --> 00:11:36,880
the challenges of building a whole processing refinery that sits on the seabed of the North Sea is particularly challenging.

156
00:11:37,800 --> 00:11:40,000
It weighed 30,000 tons.

157
00:11:41,300 --> 00:11:46,580
Its lowest level rose 84 feet above the water, to account for the North Sea's giant waves.

158
00:11:47,720 --> 00:11:54,060
The Piper was a mini city, home to over 200 men who lived and worked on the platform for weeks at a time.

159
00:11:54,061 --> 00:11:59,060
35 gas and oil wells fed into separation modules.

160
00:11:59,061 --> 00:12:03,060
A maze of pipes, tanks, pumps and valves,

161
00:12:03,061 --> 00:12:07,060
which separated the oil from the natural gas and water.

162
00:12:07,061 --> 00:12:12,060
The oil was shipped to shore in a 128-mile pipeline.

163
00:12:12,061 --> 00:12:15,060
Processing the gas was more complicated.

164
00:12:15,061 --> 00:12:20,060
The gas that came out of the formation under the Piper Alpha wasn't just methane,

165
00:12:20,061 --> 00:12:23,060
which is a very light hydrocarbon gas.

166
00:12:23,061 --> 00:12:29,500
It also contained propane, pentane, which are heavy hydrocarbon gases.

167
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:33,760
The lighter gas was sent to processing platforms connected to the piper.

168
00:12:34,840 --> 00:12:39,260
The heavier gases were condensed into liquid by applying high pressure and low temperatures.

169
00:12:40,780 --> 00:12:46,840
The liquid gas, or condensate, was then piped ashore, conveniently alongside the oil.

170
00:12:47,680 --> 00:12:52,320
Keeping the heavy gases in liquid form required the use of powerful pressurizers.

171
00:12:53,060 --> 00:12:56,440
called condensate pumps.

172
00:12:56,441 --> 00:13:01,460
On July 6, 1988, a failed valve on a condensate pump

173
00:13:01,461 --> 00:13:04,680
would trigger a disaster of extraordinary scale.

174
00:13:04,681 --> 00:13:07,040
It began with routine maintenance.

175
00:13:07,041 --> 00:13:09,940
There were several hundred valves on the platform.

176
00:13:09,941 --> 00:13:14,280
Each of these had to be checked at least once every 18 months,

177
00:13:14,281 --> 00:13:17,420
according to their technical safety requirements.

178
00:13:17,421 --> 00:13:21,260
One of the valves on one of the condensate pumps

179
00:13:21,261 --> 00:13:28,700
was off for recertification while the valve on condensate line a was down for repairs

180
00:13:29,260 --> 00:13:34,140
the redundant line b remained active but there was a problem with line b

181
00:13:35,100 --> 00:13:43,500
hydrates were forming at a high rate hydrate which occurs in processing oil coming out of the ground

182
00:13:44,460 --> 00:13:51,100
is nothing more than ice with a little hydrocarbon in it however if you think ice is an irritant

183
00:13:51,101 --> 00:13:55,540
in your house plumbing, you ought to see it form in a hydroprocessing line.

184
00:13:55,960 --> 00:13:57,680
It can totally stop the process.

185
00:13:59,140 --> 00:14:03,720
The methanol injection system, which essentially puts antifreeze into the line, had failed.

186
00:14:05,160 --> 00:14:06,880
Line B shut down automatically.

187
00:14:07,560 --> 00:14:09,500
This is very bad.

188
00:14:10,100 --> 00:14:12,860
First of all, it has grave economic consequences,

189
00:14:12,861 --> 00:14:18,500
but if you have to shut the platform down and go from what they call a black start,

190
00:14:18,501 --> 00:14:21,160
That means bring everything start from cold.

191
00:14:21,840 --> 00:14:23,480
That is a real headache.

192
00:14:23,880 --> 00:14:31,380
It was necessary to find something to do real quick to either get that pump back in service or to start the other pump.

193
00:14:31,680 --> 00:14:34,940
So they sent an electrician down to check the instrumentation.

194
00:14:35,680 --> 00:14:44,020
This particular safety valve was in an awkward position because it was located well above the pump that it protected.

195
00:14:44,021 --> 00:14:50,420
He could not see the safety valve from the position where he worked on the pump.

196
00:14:50,760 --> 00:14:53,260
He went back up and said that it was okay.

197
00:14:53,940 --> 00:14:56,980
Due to the pump's design and a lack of communication,

198
00:14:57,980 --> 00:15:01,540
the night crew didn't realize the extent to which Line A had been disabled.

199
00:15:02,940 --> 00:15:05,380
When the Line A pump was repressurized,

200
00:15:06,660 --> 00:15:11,340
gas at 1,100 PSI shot out of the valve with a deafening shriek.

201
00:15:11,341 --> 00:15:19,240
So eventually this layer of condensate gas, which now of course has become mixed with oxygen,

202
00:15:19,740 --> 00:15:24,820
and is only looking for an ignition source, finds the ignition source.

203
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:27,580
What that source was may never be known.

204
00:15:28,400 --> 00:15:34,240
But at 10 o'clock, a giant fireball ripped through the condenser module and destroyed the control room.

205
00:15:34,241 --> 00:15:42,340
The initial explosion, if that's all that would have happened, we probably would have been okay.

206
00:15:43,200 --> 00:15:52,480
But unfortunately, the initial explosion appears to have fractured the main oil line in the B module.

207
00:15:52,481 --> 00:15:57,360
And that contributed a large amount of fuel to the fire.

208
00:15:57,361 --> 00:16:03,640
oil, crude oil, happens to burn with a very thick black smoke.

209
00:16:04,180 --> 00:16:09,620
And that dense smoke basically rendered that entire deck uninhabitable very quickly.

210
00:16:10,520 --> 00:16:13,400
With the smoke making helicopter evacuation impossible,

211
00:16:14,300 --> 00:16:18,020
workers trapped above the burning production module had no good way to escape.

212
00:16:18,640 --> 00:16:25,520
So you've got to find your way in dense, dark clouds of black smoke in the middle of the night,

213
00:16:25,521 --> 00:16:31,960
find a rope and crawl 10 stories down and drop into 40-degree water and survive.

214
00:16:32,520 --> 00:16:36,580
The oil fire grew so hot that a major gas pipeline exploded.

215
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:42,800
The Piper Alpha's unmatched ability to collect and process prodigious amounts of hydrocarbon fuel

216
00:16:42,801 --> 00:16:45,720
now led to its rapid demise.

217
00:16:46,440 --> 00:16:50,480
These pressurized pipelines represent huge fuel sources

218
00:16:50,481 --> 00:16:58,160
that when they come back, literally melt the 30,000 tons of the Piper right to the waterline.

219
00:16:59,040 --> 00:17:04,940
Of the 226 people aboard the Piper Alpha, only 61 survived.

220
00:17:06,140 --> 00:17:09,440
165 workers and two rescuers died.

221
00:17:11,340 --> 00:17:15,320
The financial impact on Britain's oil industry was staggering.

222
00:17:15,321 --> 00:17:25,500
The Piper Alpha disaster still, I believe, retains the title of the most expensive man-made disaster in history.

223
00:17:26,200 --> 00:17:36,280
The year before the Piper Alpha disaster, I believe the British government took in 12.5 billion pounds in oil taxation revenue.

224
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:40,840
And in 88, 89, it was only 3.6 billion.

225
00:17:40,841 --> 00:17:44,500
After tracing the source of the fire to the failed valve,

226
00:17:45,180 --> 00:17:48,400
engineers focused on how the Piper's design flaws may have contributed

227
00:17:48,401 --> 00:17:50,320
to the fire's extreme severity.

228
00:17:51,400 --> 00:17:55,640
Among the failures, inadequate blast walls protecting the control room,

229
00:17:56,700 --> 00:17:59,160
the lack of alternative ways to shut off gas lines

230
00:17:59,161 --> 00:18:02,200
or activate fire suppression bumps,

231
00:18:02,920 --> 00:18:04,920
and insufficient exits for the workers.

232
00:18:05,820 --> 00:18:09,980
Once you called an emergency and you mustered everybody in the living quarters,

233
00:18:09,981 --> 00:18:12,800
There was no way for people to get out of there.

234
00:18:13,460 --> 00:18:19,300
In the new designs, they actually have shielded stairwells and tunnels and pathways

235
00:18:19,301 --> 00:18:23,220
where there's multiple ways out of the living quarters

236
00:18:23,221 --> 00:18:28,720
to get you down to a safe refuge and to your lifeboat stations.

237
00:18:29,460 --> 00:18:33,960
This engineering disaster forever changed the way offshore platforms operate.

238
00:18:34,980 --> 00:18:38,000
For those who design and work on these mighty structures,

239
00:18:38,001 --> 00:18:44,820
there is no more potent reminder of what can go wrong than the name Piper Alpha.

240
00:18:46,820 --> 00:18:51,360
The UK's annual natural gas production, mostly from North Sea wells,

241
00:18:51,560 --> 00:18:53,880
is greater than that of any Middle Eastern nation.

242
00:18:54,440 --> 00:18:59,200
We now return to Engineering Disasters on Modern Marvels.

243
00:19:00,460 --> 00:19:03,480
Just off Highway 2 in Willow Island, West Virginia,

244
00:19:04,460 --> 00:19:06,620
a stone's throw from the muddy Ohio River,

245
00:19:07,560 --> 00:19:08,640
stands a simple monument.

246
00:19:09,720 --> 00:19:12,700
Upon closer inspection, it is a somber memorial.

247
00:19:13,600 --> 00:19:15,760
The memorial is made out of concrete

248
00:19:15,761 --> 00:19:18,100
going up into the shape of a cooling tower.

249
00:19:18,680 --> 00:19:20,840
It has a bronze plaque on the front of it

250
00:19:20,841 --> 00:19:22,420
with the 51 names,

251
00:19:22,421 --> 00:19:25,700
and then it has a verse on the front.

252
00:19:27,520 --> 00:19:29,700
Anthony Lauer, beginning at age 12,

253
00:19:30,040 --> 00:19:32,260
spearheaded the fundraising efforts to build the memorial.

254
00:19:33,260 --> 00:19:37,720
Nine of his relatives, including his grandfather, are among the names on the plaque.

255
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:41,360
What I learned in doing the project was pretty much everything,

256
00:19:41,560 --> 00:19:45,260
because when I first started, no one ever talked about it around the house.

257
00:19:45,620 --> 00:19:48,820
Then I turned to the books, which the textbooks really didn't have much either.

258
00:19:49,280 --> 00:19:52,480
And while doing the project, I learned what happened.

259
00:19:53,540 --> 00:19:58,878
What happened on April 27, 1978, was the worst construction accident in U.S.

260
00:19:58,879 --> 00:19:59,579
history.

261
00:19:59,580 --> 00:20:02,460
and it happened less than a half mile from here.

262
00:20:03,800 --> 00:20:07,560
This is the Pleasance Power Station in Willow Island, West Virginia.

263
00:20:08,520 --> 00:20:13,720
The power plant burns coal, heating water pumped from the Ohio River to create steam,

264
00:20:14,460 --> 00:20:16,620
which turns the electricity-generating turbines.

265
00:20:17,940 --> 00:20:22,460
Before returning to the plant, the water must be cooled in these tall concrete structures

266
00:20:22,461 --> 00:20:25,080
known as natural draft cooling towers.

267
00:20:25,081 --> 00:20:30,080
The identical towers are notable for their considerable size.

268
00:20:30,081 --> 00:20:36,080
428 feet high, 359 feet wide of the base, and distinctive shape.

269
00:20:36,081 --> 00:20:44,080
We call it the hyperbolic paraboloid shape, which by the way helps it generate natural draft.

270
00:20:44,081 --> 00:20:48,080
It is an extremely difficult structure to construct.

271
00:20:48,081 --> 00:20:55,080
One cannot construct this tower using normal methods.

272
00:20:55,081 --> 00:21:06,080
In 1977, the contractor, Research Cottrell, decided to use its newly patented and highly complex lift system, called a jump form.

273
00:21:06,081 --> 00:21:12,080
It combined both scaffolding and the formwork into which concrete was poured.

274
00:21:12,081 --> 00:21:19,580
It is a traveling scaffold that's literally climbing up the constructed portion of the tower.

275
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:25,280
Unlike traditional scaffolding, the jump form system was not anchored to the ground,

276
00:21:25,940 --> 00:21:29,260
only to the previously poured forms of the concrete shell.

277
00:21:30,360 --> 00:21:34,680
The most critical components of the system are these vertical jump form beams.

278
00:21:34,681 --> 00:21:44,120
Those beams were bolted on both the outside face as well as the inside face of the concrete shell

279
00:21:44,121 --> 00:21:52,860
so that the whole assembly of scaffold on the outside and the inside would ride, so to speak, on those beams.

280
00:21:53,520 --> 00:22:01,300
To move the system up, workers unbolted the lowest beams, leapfrogged them to the top, and reattached them to the concrete shell.

281
00:22:02,140 --> 00:22:05,420
Then the entire system was jacked up by a hydraulic ram.

282
00:22:06,740 --> 00:22:11,120
As construction progressed, the cooling tower shell bore the weight of the scaffold,

283
00:22:11,980 --> 00:22:15,020
work platforms, and several dozen workers.

284
00:22:16,020 --> 00:22:19,820
You had the carpenters there moving the forming system up.

285
00:22:19,821 --> 00:22:22,740
You had iron workers there tying reinforcement bars.

286
00:22:23,240 --> 00:22:27,320
You had laborers there that were coming in behind actually pouring the concrete.

287
00:22:27,321 --> 00:22:35,320
Six cathead cranes hoisting 3,000-pound buckets of concrete were also supported by the tower shell.

288
00:22:36,320 --> 00:22:45,320
Using this complex but effective method, the construction crew had successfully completed the first tower by August of 1977.

289
00:22:46,320 --> 00:22:52,320
Work on the second tower began in the spring of 1978, as soon as the weather was warm enough.

290
00:22:52,321 --> 00:22:56,620
The crew poured concrete in five-foot-high sections called lifts.

291
00:22:57,480 --> 00:22:59,840
The goal was to pour one lift every day.

292
00:22:59,841 --> 00:23:06,380
On April 27, 1978, they had constructed 28 lifts,

293
00:23:07,100 --> 00:23:13,120
and they were about to construct, or they had started actually, to construct lift number 29.

294
00:23:14,220 --> 00:23:17,680
At 10 a.m., 51 workers were on the scaffold,

295
00:23:18,280 --> 00:23:22,200
which encircled the top of the tower 166 feet above the ground.

296
00:23:22,320 --> 00:23:29,840
As the day's third bucket of concrete neared the top, workers on the ground heard a loud, violent noise.

297
00:23:30,240 --> 00:23:36,400
It was somewhere like lightning striking right next to, you know, that horrendous crack.

298
00:23:36,940 --> 00:23:42,900
The entire scaffold and construction system began to collapse as the top layer of concrete crumbled.

299
00:23:42,901 --> 00:23:52,700
Starting at the north end of the tower, the concrete and scaffold peeled off in both directions, detaching from the rim and falling inward.

300
00:23:53,620 --> 00:23:57,860
I estimate that it was probably less than a minute, much less than a minute.

301
00:23:58,400 --> 00:24:09,680
Once it started going, its speed must have started actually increasing because of the added weight that was pulling down the rest of the scaffold.

302
00:24:09,681 --> 00:24:13,560
It brought down all 51 men with it.

303
00:24:14,900 --> 00:24:18,400
Workers on the ground ran through the shroud of dust and into the tower.

304
00:24:18,980 --> 00:24:24,440
Our first thought was to look for our fellow workers who were on that tower.

305
00:24:24,960 --> 00:24:31,240
Everyone was scrambling, looking for someone, you know, to see if there was anyone alive.

306
00:24:31,600 --> 00:24:33,300
And of course, in the end, there wasn't anyone.

307
00:24:33,301 --> 00:24:40,180
Engineers from the National Bureau of Standards and from private firms arrived to sift through

308
00:24:40,181 --> 00:24:45,600
the wreckage and determine what had caused the deadliest construction accident on American soil.

309
00:24:45,601 --> 00:24:52,380
During lab tests on the disintegrated concrete from the 28th lift, the NBS made an important

310
00:24:52,381 --> 00:24:58,985
discovery. We estimated that strength to be somewhere around 200 psi.

311
00:24:58,986 --> 00:25:00,900
Normally that before

312
00:25:00,901 --> 00:25:05,140
that construction they should have had about 800 psi concrete.

313
00:25:05,141 --> 00:25:10,260
Concrete gains strength due for two factors.

314
00:25:10,261 --> 00:25:15,540
One is the temperature condition, the other one is time.

315
00:25:15,541 --> 00:25:19,700
The temperature that night dipped down substantially low,

316
00:25:19,701 --> 00:25:23,140
so that the strength gain was much, much slower

317
00:25:23,141 --> 00:25:26,260
than the previous days.

318
00:25:26,261 --> 00:25:28,540
There's little doubt that the partially cured concrete

319
00:25:28,541 --> 00:25:30,580
had not been strong enough to hold up the scaffold.

320
00:25:31,360 --> 00:25:33,840
But other analysts trace the main cause

321
00:25:33,841 --> 00:25:36,160
to a critical failure lower on the tower.

322
00:25:37,360 --> 00:25:40,060
When we examined the beams themselves

323
00:25:40,061 --> 00:25:42,260
that were at the base of the tower,

324
00:25:42,940 --> 00:25:46,080
we found that the actual point

325
00:25:46,081 --> 00:25:47,880
where they had been connected

326
00:25:47,881 --> 00:25:49,960
or they were supposed to have been connected

327
00:25:49,961 --> 00:25:52,800
were intact and they were not deformed.

328
00:25:53,880 --> 00:25:54,960
In other words,

329
00:25:55,640 --> 00:25:57,820
some of the lower bolts had not been connected

330
00:25:57,821 --> 00:25:58,900
to the concrete at all.

331
00:25:59,900 --> 00:26:01,980
The evidence suggested that the construction crew

332
00:26:01,981 --> 00:26:04,480
had prematurely unbolted the jump form beams

333
00:26:04,481 --> 00:26:05,800
from lift 27,

334
00:26:07,020 --> 00:26:08,780
where the cured concrete would have been

335
00:26:08,781 --> 00:26:09,780
of sufficient strength.

336
00:26:11,460 --> 00:26:13,520
The forensic engineers did agree

337
00:26:13,521 --> 00:26:15,240
on two very important points.

338
00:26:16,960 --> 00:26:19,120
One was the vital importance

339
00:26:19,121 --> 00:26:20,880
of properly engineered scaffolding.

340
00:26:21,580 --> 00:26:23,660
The failed scaffold at Pleasant's power station

341
00:26:23,661 --> 00:26:25,360
brought this often overlooked problem

342
00:26:25,361 --> 00:26:26,260
to the foreground.

343
00:26:27,820 --> 00:26:32,860
In fact, our investigation showed that it was not even an engineered structure.

344
00:26:32,861 --> 00:26:39,340
It was put together by some very sharp inventor that had some good practical knowledge,

345
00:26:39,500 --> 00:26:45,740
but nothing of systematic engineering to engineer a critical component like that.

346
00:26:46,320 --> 00:26:50,400
The other point was the need for on-site testing of concrete strength.

347
00:26:50,401 --> 00:27:02,660
So now the non-destructive testing procedure in some parts of the United States must be used to determine the strength of concrete prior to removing foams.

348
00:27:03,140 --> 00:27:08,400
As a result, I don't think that we haven't seen as many of construction failures in this country.

349
00:27:09,300 --> 00:27:15,500
As for the Pleasant's cooling tower, the job was completed 18 months later using traditional scaffolding.

350
00:27:16,440 --> 00:27:20,060
But it took many more years for the emotional scars to heal.

351
00:27:20,400 --> 00:27:25,400
Since I got this done, I found that people are more open to talk about it now.

352
00:27:25,401 --> 00:27:29,400
Like, normally no one would talk about it, but now that it's complete,

353
00:27:29,401 --> 00:27:34,400
it lets people know that it's okay, we can talk about it now.

354
00:27:34,401 --> 00:27:40,400
In the end, the Pleasance cooling tower collapse resulted from the failure of two elements

355
00:27:40,401 --> 00:27:46,400
found at nearly every major construction site, concrete and scaffolding.

356
00:27:46,401 --> 00:27:52,460
The disaster was tragic proof that such elements, no matter how common, can never be taken for granted.

357
00:27:54,680 --> 00:28:01,200
Each cooling tower at the Pleasance Power Station circulates 227,000 gallons of water per minute.

358
00:28:01,840 --> 00:28:02,840
This modern...

359
00:28:02,841 --> 00:28:07,220
4, 3, 2, 1, 0.

360
00:28:08,460 --> 00:28:12,720
Humankind has been pushed to the brink of nuclear war on more than one occasion.

361
00:28:12,721 --> 00:28:22,837
And while the political reasons for these terrifying standoffs are well documented, lesser known engineering and logistical failures have come closer to setting off

362
00:28:22,838 --> 00:28:25,120
World War III than one might believe.

363
00:28:26,680 --> 00:28:37,600
Satellite miscalculations, alarm failures, communication breakdowns, and even computer bugs have nearly brought on the ultimate engineering disaster.

364
00:28:37,601 --> 00:28:43,360
Some of these incidents can be traced as far back as the Cuban Missile Crisis.

365
00:28:44,080 --> 00:28:50,480
The Cuban Missile Crisis was a seminal event, the most dangerous moment in human history.

366
00:28:51,140 --> 00:29:01,333
John Kennedy estimated at the time and afterwards that there was about a one in three chance that this confrontation would roll all the way to war, including nuclear

367
00:29:01,334 --> 00:29:02,034
war.

368
00:29:02,035 --> 00:29:07,700
The missile crisis is actually a wonderful case study for how many things can go wrong.

369
00:29:08,500 --> 00:29:13,800
Radar glitches, faulty missile tests, and unauthorized U-2 spy missions

370
00:29:13,801 --> 00:29:19,080
were just some of the technical failures that heightened the already elevated chance of disaster.

371
00:29:20,420 --> 00:29:27,860
One especially surreal incident occurred on October 25, 1962, at the height of the crisis.

372
00:29:27,861 --> 00:29:32,620
when the sighting of a shadowy figure outside a military base in Minnesota

373
00:29:32,621 --> 00:29:36,580
triggered sabotage alarms at all nearby bases.

374
00:29:37,520 --> 00:29:41,160
At Volk Field in Wisconsin, instead of the sabotage alarm,

375
00:29:41,900 --> 00:29:46,140
the klaxon alarm ordering the launch of nuclear-armed aircraft rang out.

376
00:29:47,100 --> 00:29:49,320
A squadron of planes raced down the runway

377
00:29:49,321 --> 00:29:53,400
and were within seconds of taking off when the malfunction was recognized.

378
00:29:53,700 --> 00:29:57,580
The shadowy figure at the fence turned out to be a bear.

379
00:29:57,860 --> 00:30:06,960
The Cuban Missile Crisis revealed a great deal of disorganization in the system used to manage the forces in a crisis.

380
00:30:07,520 --> 00:30:10,820
Keep in mind that this was a fairly young nuclear force.

381
00:30:11,060 --> 00:30:13,760
The missiles were just coming online.

382
00:30:14,400 --> 00:30:20,200
After the Cuban Missile Crisis had been diffused, the United States worked to streamline its nuclear command

383
00:30:20,201 --> 00:30:24,180
and improve communications among the far-flung nodes of the nuclear network.

384
00:30:24,181 --> 00:30:30,180
But one of the results of improved readiness meant going on permanent hair-trigger alert.

385
00:30:30,181 --> 00:30:37,180
We married the bombs up to the missiles and put them on basically two to three minute launch readiness.

386
00:30:37,181 --> 00:30:45,180
Meanwhile, by the 1970s, the Soviet Union had built up a formidable arsenal of submarine-launched missiles,

387
00:30:45,181 --> 00:30:49,180
some of which could reach American cities in 12 minutes.

388
00:30:49,181 --> 00:30:55,180
Even more pressure was placed on the technologies, including computers used to help human beings

389
00:30:55,181 --> 00:30:58,180
make launch decisions.

390
00:30:58,181 --> 00:31:01,320
Sometimes these technologies failed.

391
00:31:01,321 --> 00:31:07,180
As the 1970s came to a close, computer errors twice brought us dangerously close to nuclear

392
00:31:07,181 --> 00:31:09,500
conflict with the Soviet Union.

393
00:31:09,501 --> 00:31:13,400
The first occurred on November 9, 1979.

394
00:31:13,401 --> 00:31:21,680
in Colorado in an Air Force base, a simulation of a full-scale Soviet attack in a tape was

395
00:31:21,681 --> 00:31:27,980
inserted into a piece of equipment to test the equipment. And someone forgot to flip

396
00:31:27,981 --> 00:31:35,480
the switch that kept that simulated attack contained within that one piece of equipment

397
00:31:35,481 --> 00:31:36,635
on the base.

398
00:31:36,636 --> 00:31:44,240
And in failing to flip that switch, the simulated Soviet attack was transmitted

399
00:31:44,241 --> 00:31:48,700
throughout the early warning network of the United States.

400
00:31:48,701 --> 00:31:52,958
Top ranking officers convened immediately to assess the threat.

401
00:31:52,959 --> 00:31:54,580
NORAD alerted its entire

402
00:31:54,581 --> 00:31:59,242
air interceptor force, and at least 10 planes went airborne.

403
00:31:59,243 --> 00:32:02,040
Under America's prairies, minute-men

404
00:32:02,041 --> 00:32:03,880
launch silos prepared for attack.

405
00:32:06,200 --> 00:32:09,480
Among the steps that we took to get ready for nuclear war,

406
00:32:10,400 --> 00:32:16,760
crews retrieved launch keys from their safes, as well as the launch codes that would be used

407
00:32:16,761 --> 00:32:21,700
to validate a launch order, strapped into their chairs to brace for an incoming strike

408
00:32:21,701 --> 00:32:25,900
and to be ready to fire their missiles in two minutes as soon as the order came down

409
00:32:25,901 --> 00:32:27,840
from the president.

410
00:32:27,841 --> 00:32:33,760
In the ultimate sign that this threat was judged to be real, the National Emergency

411
00:32:33,761 --> 00:32:38,360
Airborne Command Post, or Doomsday Plane, also took off.

412
00:32:38,361 --> 00:32:43,660
It took eight minutes, an eternity in the world of nuclear war, for the officers huddled

413
00:32:43,661 --> 00:32:48,620
inside Cheyenne Mountain to trust their early warning satellites and decide that the massive

414
00:32:48,621 --> 00:32:51,000
Soviet attack was not real.

415
00:32:51,001 --> 00:32:54,240
It was supposed to take them only three minutes.

416
00:32:54,241 --> 00:32:59,520
The incident, which was leaked to the press, so unnerved Soviet Premier Leonid Brezhnev

417
00:32:59,521 --> 00:33:03,150
that he personally wrote President Carter, urging improvements in the U.S.

418
00:33:03,151 --> 00:33:03,851
ability to

419
00:33:03,852 --> 00:33:07,160
recognize a false alert.

420
00:33:07,161 --> 00:33:12,366
Incredibly, seven months later, another computer disaster would trick the U.S.

421
00:33:12,367 --> 00:33:13,300
into preparing

422
00:33:13,301 --> 00:33:15,840
its forces for nuclear war.

423
00:33:15,841 --> 00:33:23,820
In 1980, the false alarm was triggered by the failure of a 43-cent computer chip that

424
00:33:23,821 --> 00:33:31,740
was part of a communications device that was used to transmit from our early warning center

425
00:33:31,741 --> 00:33:35,380
in Colorado to the rest of the nuclear forces.

426
00:33:35,940 --> 00:33:42,500
The failed computer chip was in a device called a multiplexer, which transmits routine status

427
00:33:42,501 --> 00:33:48,220
information from NORAD's fortress at Cheyenne Mountain, Colorado, to the other command posts.

428
00:33:48,221 --> 00:33:56,600
The displays of the Pentagon and Strategic Air Command, or SAC, suddenly indicated a massive Soviet strike.

429
00:33:58,100 --> 00:34:03,960
This terrifying computer error looked real enough to once again order bombers to start their engines,

430
00:34:04,460 --> 00:34:08,200
and silo officers to retrieve their launch keys and strap in.

431
00:34:08,201 --> 00:34:22,201
So in 1980, the Cheyenne crew dropped the ball and failed for the second time in less than a year to come to the right conclusion that we were not under an attack in

432
00:34:22,598 --> 00:34:23,820
three minutes.

433
00:34:24,120 --> 00:34:25,060
It took them eight minutes.

434
00:34:25,840 --> 00:34:28,800
The early warning crew at Cheyenne Mountain also was fired.

435
00:34:29,620 --> 00:34:34,320
These disastrous incidents led to several key reforms in the nation's warning system.

436
00:34:35,320 --> 00:34:37,780
First, NORAD changed its computers

437
00:34:37,781 --> 00:34:41,820
so that officers in the mountain could actually see the messages

438
00:34:41,821 --> 00:34:43,740
they were sending to the Pentagon and SAC.

439
00:34:44,300 --> 00:34:50,080
In each incident, NORAD had no idea it was transmitting warnings of World War III.

440
00:34:52,880 --> 00:34:56,700
SAC's duty officers were ordered to check early warning satellites and radar

441
00:34:56,701 --> 00:34:58,560
before alerting the bomber force.

442
00:35:00,080 --> 00:35:04,160
And NORAD constructed a separate $16 million computer testing facility

443
00:35:04,161 --> 00:35:08,280
so the training tape incident would never happen again.

444
00:35:09,840 --> 00:35:12,060
As sobering as our near misses have been,

445
00:35:12,540 --> 00:35:16,360
some defense analysts worry even more about our former Cold War adversary.

446
00:35:16,660 --> 00:35:20,980
I would say that if we're making a list of worries in the nuclear arena,

447
00:35:21,540 --> 00:35:26,240
Russia's systems today should be a matter of considerable concern

448
00:35:26,241 --> 00:35:30,120
because we all know about Russia's Mir space station.

449
00:35:30,121 --> 00:35:32,540
What happened to that? It failed.

450
00:35:32,541 --> 00:35:38,780
We all know about the Russian Cursed, their most advanced new nuclear submarine, which

451
00:35:38,781 --> 00:35:42,160
on its maiden voyage sank.

452
00:35:42,161 --> 00:35:49,500
We know about this recent event in which, at Putin's test of Russia's nuclear forces,

453
00:35:49,501 --> 00:35:54,060
they had three separate occasions on which they tried to fire a missile.

454
00:35:54,061 --> 00:35:56,620
In each one of the cases, it failed.

455
00:35:56,621 --> 00:36:02,460
The concern lies not only with Russian early warning and control, which is deteriorating

456
00:36:02,461 --> 00:36:09,640
but with our own, because command and control is inherently imperfect.

457
00:36:10,420 --> 00:36:22,640
The public has become complacent and doesn't even realize today that the United States and Russia continue to operate our early warning and nuclear forces,

458
00:36:22,641 --> 00:36:31,920
thousands of them, as though we had to be prepared to fight a large-scale nuclear war with each other at the top of a hat.

459
00:36:32,460 --> 00:36:37,460
Whatever solutions we find for reducing the chance of a nuclear disaster,

460
00:36:37,461 --> 00:36:41,460
our near disasters have made one thing clear.

461
00:36:41,461 --> 00:36:45,460
We cannot rely on technology alone.

462
00:36:47,460 --> 00:36:52,980
In 1995, the test launch of a rocket in Norway appeared to Russian radar operators as a U.S.

463
00:36:52,981 --> 00:36:53,681
missile.

464
00:36:53,682 --> 00:37:01,460
The threat was taken so seriously that President Boris Yeltsin's laptop containing missile launch permission codes was activated for the first time.

465
00:37:02,460 --> 00:37:05,860
We now return to engineering disasters on Modern Marvels.

466
00:37:10,860 --> 00:37:11,660
A train wreck.

467
00:37:12,560 --> 00:37:15,760
The essence of a well-ordered system reduced to chaos.

468
00:37:16,460 --> 00:37:20,560
The very phrase train wreck has come to mean any out of control disaster.

469
00:37:24,360 --> 00:37:27,660
And although rail travel is among the very safest ways to get around,

470
00:37:28,460 --> 00:37:31,660
when a passenger train traveling at high speed does crash,

471
00:37:32,460 --> 00:37:38,120
Whether through derailment or collision, scores even hundreds of passengers can die.

472
00:37:39,320 --> 00:37:46,100
In 1964, the world was introduced to high-speed rail, with Japan's Shinkansen, or bullet train.

473
00:37:47,500 --> 00:37:51,300
The trains ran at a top speed of 133 miles per hour.

474
00:37:51,920 --> 00:38:00,180
Today, the bullet trains and France's TGV line carry passengers at speeds of up to 186 miles per hour.

475
00:38:00,181 --> 00:38:05,180
High-speed trains had proven to be a remarkably efficient means of transportation.

476
00:38:05,181 --> 00:38:10,180
And until 1998, they'd been remarkably safe.

477
00:38:10,181 --> 00:38:15,180
On June 3rd of that year, the violent crash of a German ICE high-speed train

478
00:38:15,181 --> 00:38:21,180
near the small town of Escheda highlighted the need for extremely precise engineering

479
00:38:21,181 --> 00:38:23,180
for these extremely fast trains.

480
00:38:23,181 --> 00:38:29,180
The entire railroad community was shocked when the Escheda accident took place.

481
00:38:29,181 --> 00:38:40,320
It represented not only the first fatal accident on the ICE system, but essentially it was the first significant high-speed rail accident anywhere in the world.

482
00:38:40,820 --> 00:38:49,260
The ICE, or Intercity Express trains, began service in Germany in 1991, and they were an instant hit.

483
00:38:50,500 --> 00:38:53,880
Germany had enjoyed a long love affair with speed, thanks to the Autobahn.

484
00:38:54,860 --> 00:38:59,440
The ICE trains, which reach operating speeds of up to 175 miles per hour,

485
00:38:59,620 --> 00:39:02,400
increased German train ridership by 30%.

486
00:39:02,401 --> 00:39:06,960
The trains offered a smooth, comfortable ride,

487
00:39:07,600 --> 00:39:09,600
thanks in part to a new wheel design.

488
00:39:10,080 --> 00:39:13,660
They have a piece of rubber in between the outer rim and the inner part of the wheel.

489
00:39:14,320 --> 00:39:20,180
And the purpose of that rubber is to lower the noise and the vibrations that the wheels cause.

490
00:39:20,181 --> 00:39:21,960
We call it a resilient wheel.

491
00:39:21,961 --> 00:39:26,540
This is a very common practice used on light rail vehicles, streetcars,

492
00:39:26,820 --> 00:39:31,880
but this was the first application of such a wheel on a high-speed rail train.

493
00:39:32,420 --> 00:39:37,100
The decision to use the resilient wheel design on the ICE train proved fateful.

494
00:39:37,920 --> 00:39:45,120
On the morning of June 3, 1998, ICE train 884 was traveling between Munich and Hamburg.

495
00:39:45,121 --> 00:39:52,740
The two power cars, one front and one rear, and 12 passenger cars, were speeding along

496
00:39:52,741 --> 00:39:55,460
at 112 miles per hour.

497
00:39:55,461 --> 00:40:01,120
About five minutes away from the Escheta station, passengers felt a sharp jolt, followed by jarring

498
00:40:01,121 --> 00:40:04,760
vibrations which lasted about two minutes.

499
00:40:04,761 --> 00:40:09,180
On the first passenger car, the steel tire had peeled off from the core of the resilient

500
00:40:09,181 --> 00:40:10,180
wheel.

501
00:40:10,181 --> 00:40:19,040
The tire actually fractured, came apart under the car, and hung up on part of the track brake.

502
00:40:19,400 --> 00:40:24,520
The wheel was carried along in this position down the track.

503
00:40:25,640 --> 00:40:29,580
As the train approached a series of turnout switches on its way to the Esheda station,

504
00:40:30,080 --> 00:40:36,640
the broken tire struck the guide rail of the first turnout, causing the first two cars to lean sideways.

505
00:40:37,280 --> 00:40:42,780
After that had happened, things began to go wrong very quickly.

506
00:40:43,380 --> 00:40:46,000
With the concrete overpass coming up fast,

507
00:40:46,800 --> 00:40:50,220
the careening wheels of car number one damaged the switch points,

508
00:40:50,720 --> 00:40:54,560
meaning that any one of the following cars might be diverted to the turnout track.

509
00:40:55,680 --> 00:40:59,080
Passenger car number three was the first to switch to the turnout track

510
00:40:59,081 --> 00:41:01,940
and the first to fly off the tracks completely.

511
00:41:02,760 --> 00:41:05,160
It smashed into the columns of the overpass.

512
00:41:05,161 --> 00:41:09,180
If you think about the amount of energy that was associated with that collision,

513
00:41:09,320 --> 00:41:13,920
that train weighs more than two 747s, and then going at that speed.

514
00:41:14,540 --> 00:41:17,260
The force of the collision brought the overpass down,

515
00:41:17,700 --> 00:41:22,000
crushing several train cars and causing the rest to pile up in accordion fashion.

516
00:41:22,860 --> 00:41:25,500
The degree of disintegration resembled a plane crash.

517
00:41:26,780 --> 00:41:28,660
101 passengers were killed.

518
00:41:30,120 --> 00:41:33,040
Another 105 were injured, many of them critically.

519
00:41:33,041 --> 00:41:39,180
The damaged guide rail and switch points led investigators quickly to the broken wheel.

520
00:41:40,540 --> 00:41:44,900
Further analysis found that the inner layer of rubber had increased metal fatigue

521
00:41:44,901 --> 00:41:49,960
to the point where the inside of the steel rim cracked and separated.

522
00:41:50,780 --> 00:41:55,980
Imagine if you had your bicycle and you put a piece of steel band on the outside of the bicycle wheel.

523
00:41:56,320 --> 00:41:59,240
Every time that it rolled on the ground, that piece of metal will bend,

524
00:41:59,800 --> 00:42:02,640
and that bending causes stresses on the inside of the band.

525
00:42:03,040 --> 00:42:06,420
But the flawed wheel design wasn't the only factor in this tragedy.

526
00:42:06,820 --> 00:42:11,920
Usually to have an accident, and particularly to have a severe accident, it takes a combination of two or three events.

527
00:42:12,500 --> 00:42:17,860
I mean, in the case with the ICE, you needed the wheel to fail, you needed to go over a switch,

528
00:42:17,861 --> 00:42:22,720
and you needed to have a bridge all in the wrong place to create such a severe accident.

529
00:42:23,600 --> 00:42:30,940
Nevertheless, three engineers involved in the wheel design faced 101 counts of negligent homicide in a German court.

530
00:42:31,520 --> 00:42:32,220
They were acquitted.

531
00:42:32,221 --> 00:42:34,075
It's really a technical issue.

532
00:42:34,076 --> 00:42:40,200
What caused the flaw in the wheel probably initiated as a very tiny flaw inside of the metal part.

533
00:42:40,201 --> 00:42:43,200
It is extremely hard to detect those kinds of flaws.

534
00:42:44,200 --> 00:42:51,200
After the Escheta disaster, ICE engineers reverted to the original all-steel monoblock wheel

535
00:42:51,201 --> 00:42:55,200
and installed a more advanced suspension system to reduce vibrations.

536
00:42:55,201 --> 00:42:58,200
to reduce vibrations.

537
00:42:58,201 --> 00:43:01,200
Newer train cars were also built to absorb more energy,

538
00:43:01,201 --> 00:43:05,200
thus providing passengers with a better chance of survival in a crash.

539
00:43:05,201 --> 00:43:11,200
One thing that really surprised me was the German engineer said

540
00:43:11,201 --> 00:43:15,200
that the Eschede accident would not be another Hindenburg.

541
00:43:15,201 --> 00:43:20,200
He said the Hindenburg accident basically finished the airship industry

542
00:43:20,201 --> 00:43:22,200
for Germany and the world,

543
00:43:22,201 --> 00:43:29,940
and that the German engineers were committed that this Escheda accident was not going to be a Hindenburg

544
00:43:29,941 --> 00:43:33,300
and prevent high-speed rail from being successful in Germany.

545
00:43:33,860 --> 00:43:40,300
And indeed, the Escheda disaster did not dampen the German people's fondness for the ICE and high-speed rail.

546
00:43:40,900 --> 00:43:44,680
So how do we avoid what might be called the Hindenburg syndrome,

547
00:43:45,480 --> 00:43:47,800
and actually benefit from an engineering disaster?

548
00:43:47,801 --> 00:43:55,500
It requires a careful analysis of what went wrong, as well as a bold, forward-looking redesign.

549
00:43:56,500 --> 00:44:02,180
And while lost human lives can never be recovered, the opportunity to learn and improve remains.

550
00:44:02,740 --> 00:44:12,300
Whether it's finishing the job or reinventing the wheel, engineers strive to perfect their designs and to find new and better ways to prevent disaster.

551
00:44:12,301 --> 00:44:20,120
Next on History, a neighborhood is built around a canal.

